{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788705640837,"sessionID":"ses_f88d60499ffeU18PAKsy9H7iI2","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"bash","callID":"call_9aba6c43848b45d2b09516ad","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"gh issue view 156"},"output":"title:\tGrill matcher totality/alt-composition/parser-combinator claim, round 2 -- naming convention conflict\nstate:\tOPEN\nauthor:\tkantord (Dániel Kántor)\nlabels:\t\ncomments:\t0\nassignees:\t\nprojects:\t\nmilestone:\t\nissue-type:\t\nparent:\t\nsub-issues:\t\nsub-issues-completed:\t\nblocked-by:\t\nblocking:\t\nnumber:\t156\n--\nFollow-up on matcher-totality-and-alt-design (grilled via docs/.grill/matching-and-ternary.round.yaml, 2026-08-30).\n\nMaintainer feedback on the first round (quoted verbatim, annotations mail app):\n\n> first of all i thgouth that we agree din the end that Foo is the matcher and foo is the constructor, no? so foo gives the value right away, Foo only if there is a match. also I dont knwo where circle{r} comes for a matcher, wasnt' it supposed to be Circle(r) or mayhaps Circle { r }; but basically i'm also wondering if it's necesarry to give a name to the objects we are matching if in some sense it's always `.` to begin with anyway?\n\nNo option from the original round was picked -- this is pushback on the round's own preview code, not an answer to the totality/alt-composition/parser-combinator scoping question.\n\nTwo open threads to resolve before re-asking that scoping question:\n1. The round's previews used today's actually-running lowercase `circle{r}` variant-pattern syntax (pre-migration slice). The maintainer is recalling the auto-matchers ratification (#47: matchers are first-class, tagged, capital-named) and expects pattern position to already use the capital form (`Circle(r)` / `Circle { r }`). Reconcile: is today's lowercase pattern syntax an interim step that capital-name patterns will replace, and if so is that replacement itself in scope for variant-types-flip or for this row?\n2. Whether a pattern needs to bind/name the scrutinee at all, given it's always `.` -- i.e. is `circle{r}` shorthand for something that could be spelled without ever naming the enum/variant explicitly?\n\nHuman-authored: the quoted note. Derived: the two threads, restated from it.\n","metadata":{"output":"title:\tGrill matcher totality/alt-composition/parser-combinator claim, round 2 -- naming convention conflict\nstate:\tOPEN\nauthor:\tkantord (Dániel Kántor)\nlabels:\t\ncomments:\t0\nassignees:\t\nprojects:\t\nmilestone:\t\nissue-type:\t\nparent:\t\nsub-issues:\t\nsub-issues-completed:\t\nblocked-by:\t\nblocking:\t\nnumber:\t156\n--\nFollow-up on matcher-totality-and-alt-design (grilled via docs/.grill/matching-and-ternary.round.yaml, 2026-08-30).\n\nMaintainer feedback on the first round (quoted verbatim, annotations mail app):\n\n> first of all i thgouth that we agree din the end that Foo is the matcher and foo is the constructor, no? so foo gives the value right away, Foo only if there is a match. also I dont knwo where circle{r} comes for a matcher, wasnt' it supposed to be Circle(r) or mayhaps Circle { r }; but basically i'm also wondering if it's necesarry to give a name to the objects we are matching if in some sense it's always `.` to begin with anyway?\n\nNo option from the original round was picked -- this is pushback on the round's own preview code, not an answer to the totality/alt-composition/parser-combinator scoping question.\n\nTwo open threads to resolve before re-asking that scoping question:\n1. The round's previews used today's actually-running lowercase `circle{r}` variant-pattern syntax (pre-migration slice). The maintainer is recalling the auto-matchers ratification (#47: matchers are first-class, tagged, capital-named) and expects pattern position to already use the capital form (`Circle(r)` / `Circle { r }`). Reconcile: is today's lowercase pattern syntax an interim step that capital-name patterns will replace, and if so is that replacement itself in scope for variant-types-flip or for this row?\n2. Whether a pattern needs to bind/name the scrutinee at all, given it's always `.` -- i.e. is `circle{r}` shorthand for something that could be spelled without ever naming the enum/variant explicitly?\n\nHuman-authored: the quoted note. Derived: the two threads, restated from it.\n","exit":0,"truncated":false},"title":"gh issue view 156","time":{"start":1788705640827,"end":1788705640835}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"Let me start by understanding the task. I need to:\n\n1. Read AGENTS.md at the worktree root (already provided in context, but let me verify)\n2. Run `gh issue view 156` and read every comment\n3. Read the land-failure log at ~/.cache/toylang-drive/land-gate-issue-156.log\n4. Fix the variant-checker-capital-first enforcement by gating/xfailing `every_fragment_is_a_real_program` for exactly the listed cases\n5. Make sure `just check` is green, `just fmt` and `just clippy` clean on touched code\n6. Commit on this branch per AGENTS.md with provenance line\n\nLet me start by reading the issue and the log. Let me also check the worktree structure.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_0772a0e5a001lhAA5HPMI3dPQ2","sessionID":"ses_f88d60499ffeU18PAKsy9H7iI2","messageID":"msg_07729fc73001zJlWXZIH2mj8NS"}}
